  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1241 AM CST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\nEarly this morning, some areas in the James River Valley have \nconditions that could support some localized areas of patchy fog. If \nfog were to develop, it will stick around into the morning before \ndissipating by mid-morning. The fog will cause lower visibilities at \ntimes, though visibilities should stay above one mile. High surface \npressure over SD will be moving out during the afternoon, and it \nwill continue to help to move some warm air into SD. Temperatures \ntoday will be 10-15 degrees above normal, with highs in the 50s to \nlow 60s. \n\nA low surface pressure will be pushing through SD this \nevening into the overnight hours. This low pressure will bring in \nsome precipitation along the northerly side of it, with a 15-25% \nchance for some isolated rain showers and some sprinkles over \nportions of north central and northeastern SD and west central MN \nthis evening into the early overnight hours. Not much rainfall is \nexpected from these rain showers. The ensembles forecast that there \nis only a 10-25% chance for greater than 0.01 inch of rain fall over \nportions of north central SD and far northeastern SD and west \ncentral MN. \n\nCold air will be moving in behind the surface low Wednesday through \nFriday as some high pressure moves over SD. Temperatures will cool \nduring that time to be around normal to slightly below normal \nThursday and Friday. High temperatures during that time will be in \nthe low 30s to mid 40s. The high pressure and some drier air in the \natmosphere will help precipitation to stay out. As the high pressure \nmoves out late Friday into the weekend, warmer than normal \ntemperatures start to move back in, with above normal temperatures \nSunday and Monday.",
